# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nexora — root .env (Docker Compose)
# Copy to `.env` and edit. All variables are optional unless noted.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# === Mode 1: LOCAL / DEV (default — leave everything blank) =================
# `docker compose up` → http://localhost (no HTTPS).

# === Mode 2: PUBLIC DOMAIN with auto-HTTPS ==================================
# Point your DNS A/AAAA record to this server, then:
#   DOMAIN=shop.example.com
#   ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
#   PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://shop.example.com
#   PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://shop.example.com
# Caddy will request a Let's Encrypt cert on first request.

# DOMAIN=shop.example.com
# ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://shop.example.com
# PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://shop.example.com

# === Mode 3: CLOUDFLARE TUNNEL (no open ports) ==============================
# 1) Create a tunnel in Cloudflare Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels.
# 2) Copy the token, paste below.
# 3) In CF dashboard, route hostname → http://caddy:80 (service inside compose).
# 4) Set CADDYFILE=./Caddyfile.tunnel so Caddy disables auto-HTTPS — the
#    default Caddyfile would try to issue Let's Encrypt certs and bounce
#    HTTP->HTTPS, creating a redirect loop with the tunnel.
# 5) Run with profile:  docker compose --profile tunnel up -d
# Note: CF terminates TLS, so Caddy stays on plain :80 inside the network.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=eyJh...your-long-token-here
# CADDYFILE=./Caddyfile.tunnel

# === REQUIRED in production (backend refuses to boot without these) ========
# All three are validated at boot. Run the openssl commands once on a trusted
# host and paste the output here. Rotate ORDER_TOKEN_SECRET on every release
# if you can; rotate DATABASE_ENCRYPTION_KEY ONLY with a migration plan
# (existing encrypted rows cannot be read under a different key).
#
# openssl rand -hex 32
# ORDER_TOKEN_SECRET=
#
# openssl rand -hex 32
# DATABASE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
#
# Out-of-band secret used to derive the AES key that encrypts updater
# snapshots. MUST be provisioned outside the data volume — copying the
# volume to another host then must not be enough to decrypt a snapshot.
# openssl rand -hex 32
# NEXORA_LICENSE_SECRET=

# === Admin login (REQUIRED in production) ==================================
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=please-change-me-to-a-strong-passphrase
# Or pre-hashed (argon2id) — overrides ADMIN_PASSWORD if set:
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=$argon2id$v=19$m=...$...

# === Updater + per-invoice registry (REQUIRED in production) -hex 32
# NEXORA_UPDATER_PSK=
#
# Personal Access Token issued to the customer. Used for TWO things:
#   1. Pull the per-invoice image from GHCR (needs read:packages).
#   2. Read invoices/<id>.json from the private releases repo (needs
#      `contents:read` on that ONE repo via a fine-grained PAT).
# Without it: the backend boots with paid features disabled (invoice fetch
# returns 401) and the first `docker compose pull` will fail.
# NEXORA_GHCR_TOKEN=
# GHCR_TOKEN=         # alias accepted by the updater container
#
# Override the releases repo slug if you fork (default is baked into the
# image at build time via the customer-build.yml workflow).
# NEXORA_RELEASES_REPO=8w6s/nexora-releases
# NEXORA_RELEASES_BRANCH=main

# === Litecoin xpub (paste in Admin → Settings instead, recommended) =========
# LTC_XPUB=Ltub...

# === Email provider (optional) =============================================
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# Or SMTP:
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=...
# SMTP_PASS=...

# === Port overrides (rarely needed) ================================
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443

# === Frontend build-time API origin (advanced) ==============================
# Leave blank for same-origin (recommended — Caddy routes /api/* to backend).
# Only set this if you serve the frontend on a different host than the backend.
# PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=https://api.example.com